{"interface_mentioned":true,"interface_mentioned_evidence_quote":"To address this gap, 863 cattle and 150 buffalo sampled at the interface between livestock areas and the Chobe National Park (CNP) and the Okavango Delta (OD) were screened for the presence of RVF virus (RVFV) neutralizing antibodies.","interface_mentioned_evidence_page":1,"interface_mentioned_evidence_section":"Abstract","interface_in_objectives":true,"interface_in_objectives_evidence_quote":"Given the significance of RVF to both human and animal health, this work focused on evaluating exposure to RVFV among livestock and buffalo populations at the livestock–wildlife interface in two major wildlife areas of Northern Botswana: the Okavango Delta (OD) and the CNP.","interface_in_objectives_evidence_page":2,"interface_in_objectives_evidence_section":"Introduction","conceptual_framing":true,"conceptual_framing_evidence_quote":"Our findings provide evidence that RVFV is actively circulating at the domestic and wild bovid species at the wildlife–livestock interface in the absence of other reported disease outbreaks or clinical cases.","conceptual_framing_evidence_page":5,"conceptual_framing_evidence_section":"Discussion","qualitative_representation":true,"qualitative_representation_evidence_quote":"There is no physical separation between cattle and wildlife in this region. Conversely, the OD, very rich in wildlife, is separated from livestock areas by a double veterinary cordon fence (Figure 1) to prevent contact between buffalo and cattle in the Ngamiland District (15).","qualitative_representation_evidence_page":2,"qualitative_representation_evidence_section":"Materials and Methods—Study Area","quantitative_variable_present":true,"quantitative_variable_present_evidence_quote":"Cattle Location Chobe National Park 8.5 (36/424) [6.1–11.7]

0.004

Okavango Delta 2.96 (13/439) [1.65–5.1]","quantitative_variable_present_evidence_page":4,"quantitative_variable_present_evidence_section":"Results—Table 1","variable_used_in_analysis":true,"variable_used_in_analysis_evidence_quote":"The chi-square test for homogeneity of two populations was used to evaluate the potential influence of age, sex, and capture location on the observed seroprevalence in sampled buffalo, influence of location in sampled cattle, and species and region in the total sample.","variable_used_in_analysis_evidence_page":3,"variable_used_in_analysis_evidence_section":"Materials and Methods—Statistical Analysis","interface_specific_question_addressed":true,"interface_specific_question_addressed_evidence_quote":"The overall prevalence was significantly higher (p = 0.0016) for buffalo [12.7%, 95% CI (7.8–19.5%)] than for cattle [5.7%, 95% CI (4.3–7.5%)].","interface_specific_question_addressed_evidence_page":4,"interface_specific_question_addressed_evidence_section":"Results","interface_explicit":true,"interface_explicit_evidence_quote":"Our data provide the first evidence of wide circulation of RVFV in both buffalo and cattle populations in Northern Botswana and highlight the need for further epidemiological and ecological investigations on RVF at the wildlife–livestock–human interface in this region.","interface_explicit_evidence_page":1,"interface_explicit_evidence_section":"Abstract","interface_variable_names":["species","region","location","capture location"],"interface_term_used":"livestock–wildlife interface","interface_statement_kind":"explicit","interface_statement_verbatim":"Our findings provide evidence that RVFV is actively circulating at the domestic and wild bovid species at the wildlife–livestock interface in the absence of other reported disease outbreaks or clinical cases.","interface_statement_page":5,"interface_statement_section":"Discussion","quantitative_measures":[{"measure_name":"Species (buffalo versus cattle) RVFV seroprevalence","what_it_represents":"Comparison of RVFV neutralizing-antibody prevalence between wild buffalo and domestic cattle.","measurement_method":"Sera were tested using virus neutralization test (VNT); prevalence was compared between species.","metric_or_unit":"Seroprevalence (%), 95% confidence interval, and p value.","role_in_analysis":"Species was evaluated in the total sample, including stratified analyses by region.","quantitative_result":"The overall prevalence was significantly higher in buffalo (12.7%) than cattle (5.7%; p = 0.0016).","evidence_quote":"The overall prevalence was significantly higher (p = 0.0016) for buffalo [12.7%, 95% CI (7.8–19.5%)] than for cattle [5.7%, 95% CI (4.3–7.5%)].","page":4,"section":"Results"},{"measure_name":"Cattle sampling location (CNP interface versus OD periphery)","what_it_represents":"A spatial comparison of cattle seroprevalence across the Chobe National Park interface and the Okavango Delta periphery.","measurement_method":"Cattle were sampled at crush pens selected according to proximity to protected-area boundaries; seroprevalence was compared by location.","metric_or_unit":"Seroprevalence (%), with p value.","role_in_analysis":"Location was tested as a potential influence on observed seroprevalence in sampled cattle.","quantitative_result":"Seroprevalence was higher in cattle sampled at the CNP interface (9.5%) than at the OD periphery (3.9%; p = 0.004).","evidence_quote":"Seroprevalence was significantly higher in cattle sampled at the interface with CNP compared to cattle sampled at the OD periphery (p = 0.004, 9.5 vs. 3.9%; Table 1).","page":4,"section":"Results—Cattle"}]}

